# Copy to .env.development.local for LOCAL DEVELOPMENT against the live backend.
#
# **The filename matters.** Use `.env.development.local`, NOT `.env.local`.
#
# Next loads `.env.local` for `next build` as well as `next dev`, so a dev
# override placed there is baked into the PRODUCTION bundle. That is not
# hypothetical: it happened, and Pass H caught it. `pnpm build` produced a
# bundle with `http://localhost:8100` compiled in, the gates went green on it,
# and the artifact the gates certified was not the artifact that would deploy.
# Deployed, every request would have gone to the reader's own machine and
# failed as mixed content — the exact failure the static conversion existed to
# remove.
#
# `.env.development.local` is loaded only when NODE_ENV=development, i.e. by
# `next dev`. `next build` cannot see it, so production is static by
# construction rather than by remembering.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE — where the browser gets its data.
#
#   unset / empty   read the committed static JSON tree at the same origin
#                   (frontend/public/api/**). THIS IS THE DEPLOYED BEHAVIOUR
#                   and now also the behaviour of any production build.
#
#   an origin       talk to the live FastAPI app instead, e.g.
#                   http://localhost:8100. Development only. The bodies are
#                   identical either way (both come from
#                   backend/app/payloads.py); only the URL shape differs,
#                   because a static host cannot select a file with `?res=`.
#
# Note the CORS consequence if you point a browser at some other port: the
# backend allows only http://localhost:3100 and http://127.0.0.1:3100, so a
# page served from anywhere else gets its API calls blocked and sits on the
# loading state.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE=http://localhost:8100
